Oil and petroleum hydrocarbon products are some of the ost important energy sources in the world. As long as oil is prospected, transported, stored, and used, there will be a risk of spillages that may result in significant environmental damage and vast economic loss. Oil spill cleanup costs worldwide amount to over $\$10$ billion dollars annually.1 The adverse impact to ecosystems and the long-term effects of environmental pollution by these and other releases call for an urgent need to develop new materials for cleaning up oil from impacted areas, especially because the effectiveness of oil treatment varies with time, type of oil and spill, location, and weather conditions.2 There are many adsorbents in use for oil spill cleanup, including sand, organoclays, and cotton fibers. These adsorbents, however, have a strong affinity to water, limiting their effectiveness in cleanup operations. Therefore, the development of waterproof sorbents that are effective even at a very low concentration of oil residue remains an urgent challenge. The recent Deepwater Horizon oil spill devastation raised awareness and underscored the urgent need for water-stable/-proof sorbents that can effectively remove oil residue in water, land, and air.
Metalorganic frameworks (MOFs) are promising adsorbents for many guest molecules, although reports concerning adsorption of hydrocarbon vapor in MOFs remain scarce compared to their $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ and inert gas adsorption.3 The high affinity and reactivity of many common MOFs toward water and humid air largely limit their open-air applications.4 Thus, the search for water-stable and hydrophobic MOFs combining good thermal stability, high selectivity, and excellent recyclability is a major challenge of great technological importance for oil spill cleanup, hydrocarbon storage in a solid matrix to allow transportation in smaller and safer vehicles, catalysis, water purification, component/isomer separation from gasoline mixtures, greenhouse gas remediation, etc. Fluorous metal organic frameworks (FMOFs) are a new class of advanced porous material that we reported recently, whose pore surface is fluorine-lined and hydrogen-free that endows greater stability and more favorable adsorption properties vs nonfluorinated or partially fluorinated MOFs.5,6 The hydrophobic character of the perfluorinated inner surface of FMOFs offers unprecedented potential for enhancing and fine-tuning the affinity for oil adsorbates in the presence of water or moist air. FMOF-1 was the first example of FMOFs, constructed from silver(I) 3,5-bis(trifluoromethyl)-1,2,4-triazolate $(\mathrm{AgTz})$ .5 Here we show that FMOF-1 and FMOF-2 (Chart 1) are highly hydrophobic and efficient for adsorption of typical aromatic and aliphatic oil components. This is a significant finding given that the confinement and interaction of water in hydrophobic space represent an important issue in science and technology7 that has attracted increasing interest in the past decade, including water adsorption in activated carbons and all-silica zeolites,8 functionalized or coated mesoporous materials,9 and biological channels.10
The small cages around the main channels in FMOF-1 are large enough to accommodate gas molecules such as ${\bf N}_{2},$ $\mathrm{~O}_{2},$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2},$ with one molecule per cage,5,6 but are too small to include oil components like toluene and hexanes. Enlarging the cage size around the channels, therefore, could potentially boost the oil capacity of such hydrophobic frameworks. It has been reported that phase transition via annealing is a good strategy to obtain new thermally stable porous crystals.17 We have successfully isolated such a framework, $\bar{[}\mathrm{Ag}(\mathrm{Ag}_{3}\bar{\mathrm{Tz}}_{4})]_{3/2^{\bullet}}4\mathrm{T}$ oluene (FMOF-2•4Toluene; Figure 3), by annealing FMOF-1 at ca. $300^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ overnight and then reassembling the framework from toluene/acetonitrile, effectively doubling the toluene solvate content per $\mathrm{AgTz}$ unit. The structure of FMOF- $2^{18}$ consists of a sub-building unit $\left[{\tt A g}_{3}{\tt T z}_{4}\right]$ with tw $\mathsf{\Omega}_{3}[\mathrm{Ag}_{2}\mathrm{Tz}_{2}]$ plane moieties (Chart 1 and Figure 3a) that are perpendicular to each other and interconnected via sharing the four-coordinate $\mathrm{Ag}(1)$ . The $\mathrm{Ag}(2)$ atom in each of the plane moieties is three-coordinate and connects to the adjacent $\mathrm{[Ag_{3}T z_{4}]}$ units to form 2D grid sheets (porous layers) parallel to the $a b$ plane. These layers are then interconnected vertically via the two-coordinate $\mathrm{Ag}(3)$ atoms $(\mathrm{Ag}(3)-\mathrm{N}(6),2.118(8)\mathring\mathrm{A})$ along the $c$ -axis to form a 3-D porous network with $\{\mathrm{Ag}[\mathrm{Ag}_{3}\mathrm{Tz}_{4}]\}_{3/2}$ repeat units in $P6/m$ space group symmetry. Two types of voids are observed for the 3-D porous network along the $\boldsymbol{\mathscr{c}}$ -axis: microporous hexagonal channels, about $1.8\mathrm{nm}$ in width, and triangular-shaped nanocages with pores about $1.0\ \mathrm{nm}$ in diameter surrounding the channels (Figure 3b). The defining features of the cages are their two-gate openings exposed to adjacent cages along the $c$ -axis, each consisting of three flexible $\mathrm{CF}_{3}$ groups that provide communication between the cages. Both the large channels and small cages are filled by toluene molecules, with 3 and 6 molecules per cage and channel in each layer, respectively. Toluene molecules whose planes are parallel to the $c$ -axis are arranged as propeller blades around the $\mid c\mid$ -axis to give idealized $C_{3}$ - and $C_{6}$ -symmetries in the cage and channel, respectively. The toluene solvate of FMOF-2 can be formulated accordingly as $\left[\mathrm{Ag}(\mathrm{Ag}_{3}\mathrm{Tz}_{4})\right]_{3/2}{\bullet4}$ Toluene. The toluene content in FMOF-2 is doubled as compared to FMOF-1, giving an adsorption limit of 12 toluene molecules within the unit cell of $5799.8\mathring{\mathrm{A}}^{\widehat{3}}$ .
In summary, we have demonstrated superhigh hydrophobic FMOFs exhibiting remarkable air and water stability and a high capacity with high affinity to $C_{6}\mathrm{-C_{8}}$ hydrocarbons of oil components. These FMOFs can selectively adsorb $C_{6}\mathrm{-C_{8}}$ hydrocarbons in preference to water, through a combination of hydrophobicity and capillary action. Our results suggest that the FMOFs represent a promising class of porous materials that should find practical applications in the removal of organics, particularly in the field of oil spill cleanup and hydrocarbon storage. While available FMOFs are stable when exposed to water and air and can be readily recycled many times, the practicality of their use and mass production considerations will increase upon expansion to include abundant metals such as $\mathrm{Cu}$ and $Z\mathrm{n}$ instead of Ag.
